**How hashing works.** A hash table maps keys to array indexes. A **hash function** converts a key to an integer, and `index = hash(key) mod capacity` selects a bucket. A good hash function is deterministic and spreads keys uniformly. Insert, lookup and delete are **O(1) on average**.

**Collisions** (two keys, same index) are unavoidable. Two main resolutions:
- **Chaining:** each bucket holds a list (or a balanced tree in Java 8+ when long). Worst case O(n) if all keys land in one bucket.
- **Open addressing:** probe other slots (linear, quadratic, double hashing) until a free one is found. Needs a low load factor and care with deletions (tombstones).

The **load factor** (n / capacity) controls performance. When it exceeds a threshold (for example 0.75), the table **resizes** (doubles) and rehashes all keys, giving amortised O(1) inserts. Keys used in a hash table must have consistent `equals` and `hashCode`.

**Heap sort.** Uses a binary heap stored in an array: children of index `i` are `2i+1` and `2i+2`.
1. **Build a max-heap** from the array by calling `heapify` (sift down) on each non-leaf node from `n/2 - 1` down to 0. This is O(n).
2. **Repeat:** swap the root (the maximum) with the last element of the heap, shrink the heap size by one, and sift down the new root. Do this n-1 times, each O(log n).

```java
void heapSort(int[] a) {
    int n = a.length;
    for (int i = n / 2 - 1; i >= 0; i--) siftDown(a, n, i);
    for (int end = n - 1; end > 0; end--) {
        int t = a[0]; a[0] = a[end]; a[end] = t;
        siftDown(a, end, 0);
    }
}
void siftDown(int[] a, int n, int i) {
    while (true) {
        int l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, big = i;
        if (l < n && a[l] > a[big]) big = l;
        if (r < n && a[r] > a[big]) big = r;
        if (big == i) return;
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[big]; a[big] = t;
        i = big;
    }
}
```

Time is O(n log n) in all cases, space O(1) extra (in place), and it is **not stable**.
